course: dictionary
lesson: 6563
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one you need. Nick is a good example. It can mean a small cut, it can mean to steal, and it can be part of a phrase like «in the nick of time».
explain: «Someone nicked my bike!» Nicked here means stolen, because the speaker has lost a bike. Always ask: what word would fit here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: «Someone's nicking his chips.» Nicking is British slang for stealing, so someone is taking the chips. Be careful with a capital N: Nick Wilde is a name, the fox in Zootopia, not the word nick.
fact: A nick is a small cut or notch on a surface or the skin.
fact: To nick something means to make a small cut in it.
fact: In British, Irish, Australian and New Zealand slang, «nick» means to steal, as in «Someone nicked my bike!»
fact: «In the nick of time» means only just in time.
fact: «In good nick» means in good condition, in British slang.
exercise: In first aid, a small nick needs washing and a plaster. What is a nick here? | A cheap bargain | A police station | *A small cut | A tune played loudly
exercise: «Someone's nicking his chips.» What does nicking mean? | Eating slowly | Sharing | Cooking | *Stealing
exercise: The hero arrived «in the nick of time». What does that mean? | Wearing a plaster on her hand | *Only just in time | Very late, after the party had ended | In a shop
test: «The old car was in good nick, with no rust at all.» What does in good nick mean? | Very cheap | Cut into pieces | Stolen | *In good condition
test: Mia says nick always means a cut. Is she right? | Yes, but only in Scotland | No, it is the name of a fox | Yes, nick always means a cut | *No, it can also mean steal, or only just in time
exam: In «Someone nicked my bike!», what does nicked mean? | Sold for a fair price | Cut in two | *Stolen | Given back
